      "id": 1,
      "text": "Greer was founded in 1879 by Mormon pioneers from Utah, including Willard Lee's family, who first called the area Lee's Valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greer%2C_Arizona"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1898 the post office renamed the town Greer after Americus Vespucius Greer to shorten the name for postal purposes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Greer,_Arizona"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The first one-room schoolhouse appeared in 1897, and a local LDS branch was formally established in 1896, shaping the town’s early social life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Greer,_Arizona"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Greer sits at about 8,400 feet, making it Arizona’s highest town and a gateway to the White Mountains.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greer%2C_Arizona"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The White Mountain Scenic Railroad ran through Greer from 1918 to 1976, transporting logs and tourists along the rugged ridge.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
